WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.620
Hey, it's another episode of Tesla Short's Time Daily.

00:00:02.980 --> 00:00:06.220
Number 433, April 11th, 2026, I'm Patrick,

00:00:06.440 --> 00:00:08.400
coming to you from a drizzly Vancouver morning.

00:00:08.520 --> 00:00:11.780
Let's catch up on what's actually moving at Tesla right now.

00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:15.220
The big one everyone's buzzing about is that Tesla has finally

00:00:15.220 --> 00:00:18.200
opened its dedicated semi-factory in Sparks, Nevada.

00:00:18.620 --> 00:00:20.140
This isn't just another ribbon-cutting,

00:00:20.140 --> 00:00:22.680
it's a purpose-built line aimed at eventually pushing

00:00:22.680 --> 00:00:24.300
toward 50,000 trucks a year.

00:00:24.800 --> 00:00:26.880
For the longest time, semi-production was squeezed

00:00:26.880 --> 00:00:29.980
into the same facilities that build Model 3s and Ys,

00:00:30.040 --> 00:00:32.780
which meant every time they wanted to make another electric class,

00:00:33.080 --> 00:00:36.100
eight, they had to shuffle resources away from passenger cars.

00:00:36.460 --> 00:00:39.260
That shared space-reality created real bottlenecks,

00:00:39.340 --> 00:00:42.300
not just in floor space, but in tooling, supply chains,

00:00:42.560 --> 00:00:43.960
and even worker specialization.

00:00:44.600 --> 00:00:47.540
Now, with its own home, the semi-line can be optimized

00:00:47.540 --> 00:00:49.800
from the ground up for heavy-duty assembly,

00:00:50.440 --> 00:00:52.460
thicker frames, massive battery packs,

00:00:52.640 --> 00:00:53.880
and the unique thermal demands

00:00:53.880 --> 00:00:56.700
that come with hauling freight across long distances.

00:00:57.180 --> 00:01:00.320
From a business angle, this feels like Tesla is putting real money

00:01:00.320 --> 00:01:03.040
behind the belief that fleet adoption is accelerating,

00:01:03.040 --> 00:01:05.900
especially among big operators who see the total cost

00:01:05.900 --> 00:01:08.840
of ownership math starting to flip in favor of electric.

00:01:09.360 --> 00:01:11.860
Margins on these trucks could look quite different

00:01:11.860 --> 00:01:13.160
from the consumer side.

00:01:13.420 --> 00:01:15.960
Higher utilization rates, longer duty cycles,

00:01:16.380 --> 00:01:19.820
and customers who care more about uptime than flashy features.

00:01:20.360 --> 00:01:22.840
It also carves out a stronger position for Tesla

00:01:22.840 --> 00:01:24.740
in the commercial energy ecosystem,

00:01:24.920 --> 00:01:27.780
where a semi can double as a rolling battery on a job site.

00:01:28.260 --> 00:01:31.320
The factory tour footage that started circulating this week

00:01:31.320 --> 00:01:32.540
makes it feel tangible.

00:01:33.180 --> 00:01:35.680
You can actually see trucks moving down the line

00:01:35.680 --> 00:01:37.060
instead of concept renders.

00:01:37.400 --> 00:01:40.500
That kind of visibility matters because it shifts the conversation

00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:42.520
from if to how fast.

00:01:42.940 --> 00:01:45.420
I'll be honest, it's the kind of manufacturing maturity

00:01:45.420 --> 00:01:47.240
that Tesla has been chasing for years,

00:01:47.760 --> 00:01:50.360
and it couldn't come at a better time with demand signals

00:01:50.360 --> 00:01:52.960
picking up from logistics fleets across the US.

00:01:53.460 --> 00:01:55.640
That factory momentum leads pretty naturally

00:01:55.640 --> 00:01:58.580
into what just happened on the regulatory side in Europe,

00:01:58.960 --> 00:02:02.060
because scaling trucks and scaling autonomy both run straight

00:02:02.060 --> 00:02:03.540
into the same approval walls.

00:02:03.980 --> 00:02:06.080
Dutch regulators have now given the green light

00:02:06.080 --> 00:02:08.600
to Tesla's supervised full self-driving software.

00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:11.000
The first time the feature has received any form

00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:12.900
of type approval anywhere in Europe.

00:02:13.580 --> 00:02:16.920
The RDW, which is basically the Dutch vehicle authority,

00:02:17.360 --> 00:02:20.040
granted provisional validity for European type approval,

00:02:20.040 --> 00:02:22.860
and the expectation is that owners in the Netherlands

00:02:22.860 --> 00:02:25.900
with an active FSD subscription will start seeing

00:02:25.900 --> 00:02:28.400
the software update roll out in the coming days.

00:02:28.960 --> 00:02:31.080
This is a bigger deal than it might sound at first.

00:02:31.780 --> 00:02:35.100
Europe has been notoriously cautious, some would say slow,

00:02:35.420 --> 00:02:38.100
when it comes to advanced driver assistance systems,

00:02:38.280 --> 00:02:40.700
especially anything with the word autonomy attached.

00:02:41.240 --> 00:02:44.140
Years of back and forth data requests and safety audits

00:02:44.140 --> 00:02:46.720
have left Tesla playing catch-up on that side of the Atlantic.

00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:49.000
A supervised approval in the Netherlands

00:02:49.000 --> 00:02:50.260
could act as a beachhead.

00:02:50.540 --> 00:02:52.080
If it holds up in real-world use,

00:02:52.280 --> 00:02:54.840
it becomes precedent that other member states can lean on.

00:02:55.480 --> 00:02:58.840
What I find refreshing is how transparent the Dutch authority has been.

00:02:59.000 --> 00:03:01.560
They laid out exactly what the system can and cannot do

00:03:01.560 --> 00:03:03.460
where the limitations sit

00:03:03.460 --> 00:03:05.880
and what drivers are still responsible for.

00:03:06.260 --> 00:03:09.600
That kind of clear communication sets realistic expectations

00:03:09.600 --> 00:03:11.800
instead of letting hype fill the vacuum.

00:03:12.240 --> 00:03:13.700
For Tesla, the timing is helpful.

00:03:14.040 --> 00:03:15.800
While the semi-factory scales hardware,

00:03:15.800 --> 00:03:19.200
getting supervised FSD into Europe starts to prove

00:03:19.200 --> 00:03:21.740
the software side of the business on a new continent.

00:03:22.300 --> 00:03:25.580
It won't turn every model Y into a robot taxi overnight,

00:03:25.580 --> 00:03:27.780
but it chips away at the regulatory moat

00:03:27.780 --> 00:03:30.160
that's kept the feature mostly North American so far.

00:03:31.060 --> 00:03:33.260
I keep thinking this is the pattern we've seen before.

00:03:33.800 --> 00:03:36.420
One country cracks the door, the data comes in,

00:03:36.660 --> 00:03:38.620
and the rest of the region starts to follow

00:03:38.620 --> 00:03:40.240
once the risk feels manageable.

00:03:41.520 --> 00:03:44.080
Speaking of different continents telling different stories,

00:03:44.080 --> 00:03:47.120
the early 2026 sales numbers out of the U.S.

00:03:47.180 --> 00:03:49.880
are painting a pretty clear picture of divergence.

00:03:50.700 --> 00:03:54.460
Overall, electric vehicle sales in the American market are declining,

00:03:55.060 --> 00:03:58.040
yet Tesla is the outlier showing actual growth

00:03:58.040 --> 00:03:59.760
while most traditional automakers

00:03:59.760 --> 00:04:03.280
and even some newer EV players saw their numbers drop.

00:04:04.300 --> 00:04:06.160
It's the kind of split that makes you pause.

00:04:06.660 --> 00:04:10.800
A lot of brands loaded up on inventory expecting continued strong demand,

00:04:10.800 --> 00:04:12.840
only to run into higher interest rates,

00:04:13.160 --> 00:04:15.300
range anxiety, and certain parts of the country,

00:04:15.740 --> 00:04:18.260
and buyers who got sticker shock once incentives

00:04:18.260 --> 00:04:20.340
and fuel savings were fully calculated.

00:04:20.960 --> 00:04:23.300
Tesla, for its part, seems to be holding up better,

00:04:23.640 --> 00:04:26.140
partly because it's product mix, software revenue,

00:04:26.320 --> 00:04:27.920
and direct sales model give it levers

00:04:27.920 --> 00:04:29.620
that legacy dealers don't have.

00:04:30.360 --> 00:04:32.120
Vertical integration helps here too.

00:04:32.620 --> 00:04:34.640
When battery costs or supply chains tighten,

00:04:35.080 --> 00:04:37.000
Tesla can adjust faster than companies

00:04:37.000 --> 00:04:39.140
still reliant on outside suppliers.

00:04:39.140 --> 00:04:41.840
This resilience in a softening market is exactly

00:04:41.840 --> 00:04:43.460
why the company is leaning harder

00:04:43.460 --> 00:04:45.640
into more affordable vehicles right now.

00:04:46.080 --> 00:04:49.500
Word is, they're actively working on a lower priced electric SUV

00:04:49.500 --> 00:04:52.260
that could sit below the current model Y in the lineup.

00:04:52.880 --> 00:04:54.080
The goal is pretty straightforward.

00:04:54.600 --> 00:04:58.160
Bring in buyers who have been priced out of the existing Tesla range,

00:04:58.280 --> 00:05:00.200
but who still want to make the switch from gas.

00:05:00.760 --> 00:05:03.660
It's a deliberate pivot toward volume over pure margin

00:05:03.660 --> 00:05:04.560
in certain segments,

00:05:04.880 --> 00:05:07.280
especially as competitors flood the mid-price space

00:05:07.280 --> 00:05:08.520
with their own crossovers.

00:05:09.140 --> 00:05:10.420
Getting the cost structure right

00:05:10.420 --> 00:05:12.440
on a more accessible vehicle isn't trivial.

00:05:13.140 --> 00:05:15.060
Batteries, motors, and interior quality

00:05:15.060 --> 00:05:17.920
all have to come down without killing the ownership experience

00:05:17.920 --> 00:05:19.340
that Tesla owners expect.

00:05:19.660 --> 00:05:22.040
If they pull it off, it could stabilize deliveries

00:05:22.040 --> 00:05:24.100
and broaden the brand's appeal at a time

00:05:24.100 --> 00:05:26.720
when the overall EV market feels a bit wobbly.

00:05:27.120 --> 00:05:29.900
I see it as Tesla acknowledging that while robot taxis

00:05:29.900 --> 00:05:31.620
and energy storage are the long game,

00:05:32.060 --> 00:05:33.780
they still need healthy vehicle sales

00:05:33.780 --> 00:05:35.920
in the near term to fund everything else.

00:05:36.340 --> 00:05:38.840
That focus on future products is also showing up

00:05:38.840 --> 00:05:41.080
in some visible changes to the cybercab.

00:05:41.420 --> 00:05:43.200
Recent sightings of prototypes reveal

00:05:43.200 --> 00:05:45.820
that the latest version now includes a steering wheel,

00:05:46.160 --> 00:05:47.900
which is a pretty noticeable walk back

00:05:47.900 --> 00:05:49.620
from the original vision of a vehicle

00:05:49.620 --> 00:05:51.480
with no steering wheel or pedals at all.

00:05:52.100 --> 00:05:54.820
Tesla hasn't put out an official statement explaining the shift,

00:05:55.160 --> 00:05:57.760
but the reasoning feels pretty obvious when you think about it.

00:05:58.160 --> 00:06:00.460
Most major markets still require some form

00:06:00.460 --> 00:06:02.260
of manual control as a fallback,

00:06:02.620 --> 00:06:04.040
especially while supervised

00:06:04.040 --> 00:06:06.960
or unsupervised autonomy is being proven at scale.

00:06:07.740 --> 00:06:10.580
Regulators want to know that if the computers get confused,

00:06:10.840 --> 00:06:11.900
a human can step in.

00:06:12.280 --> 00:06:14.780
Adding the wheelback likely reflects the reality

00:06:14.780 --> 00:06:16.800
that truly driverless operation

00:06:16.800 --> 00:06:19.340
without any manual controls is still years away

00:06:19.340 --> 00:06:20.160
in many jurisdictions,

00:06:20.180 --> 00:06:22.200
even if the software is getting close.

00:06:22.500 --> 00:06:24.620
It raises practical questions about timelines,

00:06:24.980 --> 00:06:28.100
liability, and how manufacturers balance bold visions

00:06:28.100 --> 00:06:30.220
with the incremental safety requirements

00:06:30.220 --> 00:06:31.380
that governments impose.

00:06:31.920 --> 00:06:35.200
On one hand, it might disappoint the hardcore autonomy believers

00:06:35.200 --> 00:06:37.540
who wanted the pure robo-taxi look.

00:06:38.060 --> 00:06:40.340
On the other, it shows Tesla is willing to adapt

00:06:40.340 --> 00:06:42.580
when real-world data and regulatory feedback

00:06:42.580 --> 00:06:43.760
suggest a different path.

00:06:44.340 --> 00:06:46.480
I've always thought these visible changes are healthy.

00:06:46.780 --> 00:06:48.580
They suggest the company is learning rather

00:06:48.580 --> 00:06:51.080
than stubbornly sticking to a 2020 render.

00:06:51.660 --> 00:06:53.480
The CyberCab still looks futuristic

00:06:53.480 --> 00:06:56.740
just a bit more grounded in today's legal and safety environment.

00:06:57.300 --> 00:07:00.080
It'll be interesting to see whether this becomes the production spec

00:07:00.080 --> 00:07:02.160
or if it's purely a development mule

00:07:02.160 --> 00:07:04.940
to gather more miles while the software catches up.

00:07:05.460 --> 00:07:07.600
That regulatory and timeline reality

00:07:07.600 --> 00:07:10.180
is probably part of what's behind the upbeat note

00:07:10.180 --> 00:07:12.000
that came out from Morgan Stanley this week.

00:07:12.540 --> 00:07:14.960
Their analysts took a largely positive view

00:07:14.960 --> 00:07:16.900
on Tesla's robo-taxi ambitions

00:07:16.900 --> 00:07:19.380
and the upcoming full-self-driving version 15,

00:07:19.940 --> 00:07:21.920
pointing to expected jumps and capability

00:07:21.920 --> 00:07:24.100
and the potential for meaningful deployment

00:07:24.100 --> 00:07:24.880
in the coming years.

00:07:25.460 --> 00:07:28.000
The report spends time on how these technologies

00:07:28.000 --> 00:07:30.520
could open entirely new revenue streams

00:07:30.520 --> 00:07:33.200
that go well beyond one-time vehicle sales,

00:07:33.600 --> 00:07:36.820
think utilization-based earnings from autonomous operation.

00:07:37.580 --> 00:07:40.760
Elon Musk himself confirmed that the next major FSD release

00:07:40.760 --> 00:07:42.820
will bring roughly a 10-fold increase

00:07:42.820 --> 00:07:44.080
in neural network parameters

00:07:44.080 --> 00:07:46.760
which should help the system handle more edge cases

00:07:46.760 --> 00:07:49.200
and make better decisions in complex environments.

00:07:49.760 --> 00:07:51.020
From a business perspective,

00:07:51.300 --> 00:07:53.540
having a major Wall Street firm frame autonomy

00:07:53.540 --> 00:07:55.560
as a credible growth driver helps counter

00:07:55.560 --> 00:07:57.900
some of the near-term pressure on vehicle margins,

00:07:58.420 --> 00:07:59.940
it gives investors a different lens

00:07:59.940 --> 00:08:03.120
to look through, especially when quarterly car sales fluctuate.

00:08:03.820 --> 00:08:05.960
Of course, analysts have been optimistic before

00:08:05.960 --> 00:08:09.040
so the real test will be whether version 15 delivers

00:08:09.040 --> 00:08:11.480
the kind of leap that turns supervised driving

00:08:11.480 --> 00:08:13.960
into something that feels dramatically more capable.

00:08:14.500 --> 00:08:16.540
Still, it's the kind of external validation

00:08:16.540 --> 00:08:19.600
that Tesla doesn't mind seeing heading into earnings season

00:08:19.600 --> 00:08:21.600
and yet the stock market isn't exactly

00:08:21.600 --> 00:08:23.600
sharing that enthusiasm right now.

00:08:24.080 --> 00:08:26.880
Tesla shares have declined for eight consecutive weeks

00:08:26.880 --> 00:08:29.280
as we head into the upcoming earnings report.

00:08:29.940 --> 00:08:33.300
That kind of sustained slide reflects a mix of investor caution,

00:08:33.780 --> 00:08:37.060
worries about near-term demand, increased competition,

00:08:37.340 --> 00:08:41.280
and the long wait until autonomy starts throwing off serious cash.

00:08:42.180 --> 00:08:44.260
It's easy to get caught up in the red candles

00:08:44.260 --> 00:08:46.200
but I keep coming back to the same thought.

00:08:46.640 --> 00:08:49.340
The stock price is one signal, not the only one.

00:08:49.920 --> 00:08:52.440
What actually matters more is what management says

00:08:52.440 --> 00:08:54.880
on the call about factor utilization rates,

00:08:55.140 --> 00:08:57.160
the pace of full self-driving improvement

00:08:57.160 --> 00:08:59.940
and how quickly that new semi-line can ramp.

00:09:00.380 --> 00:09:03.080
Those operational details tend to tell a true or story

00:09:03.080 --> 00:09:04.340
about where the company is headed

00:09:04.340 --> 00:09:05.800
than any single week's trading.

00:09:06.100 --> 00:09:07.760
The disconnect between the share price

00:09:07.760 --> 00:09:10.560
and the visible progress on trucks and software

00:09:10.560 --> 00:09:11.800
is something we've seen before.

00:09:12.020 --> 00:09:15.320
It usually resolves once concrete milestones start stacking up.

00:09:15.920 --> 00:09:17.260
One of those concrete milestones

00:09:17.260 --> 00:09:19.460
is exactly what Ashley Vance captured

00:09:19.460 --> 00:09:22.260
on his recent tour of the new semi-factory in Sparks.

00:09:22.840 --> 00:09:25.480
The footage he brought back shows real production flow,

00:09:25.480 --> 00:09:29.260
battery marriage stations, light tunnels for quality inspection,

00:09:29.500 --> 00:09:32.620
and actual class eight trucks moving down active assembly lines

00:09:32.620 --> 00:09:34.200
rather than sitting on display.

00:09:34.620 --> 00:09:37.980
That's a meaningful step up from the limited pilot builds

00:09:37.980 --> 00:09:39.620
we saw in previous years.

00:09:39.880 --> 00:09:42.020
It gives both potential fleet customers

00:09:42.020 --> 00:09:44.880
and investors something tangible to look at

00:09:44.880 --> 00:09:46.480
instead of just PowerPoint slides.

00:09:46.840 --> 00:09:48.240
You can see the specialized tooling

00:09:48.240 --> 00:09:50.360
that only makes sense once you've committed

00:09:50.360 --> 00:09:51.980
to high volume truck production.

00:09:52.660 --> 00:09:54.020
For me, this kind of visibility

00:09:54.020 --> 00:09:56.260
reduces some of the trust us element

00:09:56.260 --> 00:09:59.460
that has sometimes surrounded Tesla's heavier commercial products.

00:09:59.780 --> 00:10:01.880
It should help build confidence heading into earnings,

00:10:01.880 --> 00:10:04.560
especially if management can give realistic numbers

00:10:04.560 --> 00:10:07.120
around how quickly they think they can scale from here.

00:10:07.960 --> 00:10:10.300
The factory itself finally feels like it has momentum

00:10:10.300 --> 00:10:12.940
and that momentum is probably the best antidote

00:10:12.940 --> 00:10:14.640
to eight weeks of falling share prices.

00:10:15.260 --> 00:10:17.620
On the regulatory side, the Dutch vehicle authority

00:10:17.620 --> 00:10:19.400
followed up their approval by posting

00:10:19.400 --> 00:10:21.440
a clear plain language breakdown

00:10:21.440 --> 00:10:25.380
of exactly how the provisional European FSD approval works

00:10:25.380 --> 00:10:27.320
and what limitations still apply.

00:10:27.860 --> 00:10:31.100
That kind of transparency is rare and genuinely useful.

00:10:31.840 --> 00:10:34.320
It tells owners what the system is approved for today

00:10:34.320 --> 00:10:36.340
where a driver must remain attentive

00:10:36.340 --> 00:10:39.900
and what data is being collected to support future expansions.

00:10:40.120 --> 00:10:42.500
In a space where hype often outruns reality,

00:10:42.980 --> 00:10:45.460
this kind of official context helps everyone

00:10:45.460 --> 00:10:47.440
keep their expectations calibrated.

00:10:48.080 --> 00:10:49.280
Elsewhere in the ecosystem,

00:10:49.280 --> 00:10:52.360
a third-party operator is putting up more chargers compatible

00:10:52.360 --> 00:10:56.000
with Tesla's network in the Southern California city of Temecula.

00:10:56.560 --> 00:10:59.360
On the surface, it might seem like a small local story,

00:10:59.560 --> 00:11:01.120
but it's actually a pretty good signal.

00:11:01.900 --> 00:11:05.480
The charging network is no longer just Tesla-owned stations.

00:11:06.200 --> 00:11:07.880
It's starting to grow through partnerships

00:11:07.880 --> 00:11:10.320
and third-party builds in suburban areas

00:11:10.320 --> 00:11:12.120
that were previously underserved.

00:11:12.420 --> 00:11:15.000
For owners, that means one more convenient stop

00:11:15.000 --> 00:11:18.200
on longer trips or even daily errands and growing communities.

00:11:18.200 --> 00:11:20.840
It's the kind of quiet infrastructure expansion

00:11:20.840 --> 00:11:24.240
that makes electric ownership incrementally easier over time.

00:11:24.820 --> 00:11:26.520
Of course, not everything is expansion.

00:11:26.880 --> 00:11:28.760
The competitive pressure is getting more direct.

00:11:29.820 --> 00:11:33.040
Polestar is now offering discounts of up to $21,000

00:11:33.040 --> 00:11:35.820
specifically for Tesla owners who switch brands

00:11:35.820 --> 00:11:39.160
with even deeper savings available if you're a Costco member.

00:11:39.620 --> 00:11:41.820
That's an aggressive move aimed squarely

00:11:41.820 --> 00:11:43.720
at Tesla's existing customer base.

00:11:44.280 --> 00:11:46.580
In a market where volume is harder to come by,

00:11:46.580 --> 00:11:48.960
some rivals have decided the fastest path

00:11:48.960 --> 00:11:52.760
is to lure away people who have already bought into the EV lifestyle.

00:11:53.360 --> 00:11:55.780
It illustrates how intense things have become

00:11:55.780 --> 00:11:57.460
in the premium electric segment.

00:11:57.660 --> 00:11:59.560
Price is now very much on the table.

00:12:00.180 --> 00:12:05.180
At the same time, early 2026 figures suggest Toyota's new BZ electric SUV

00:12:05.180 --> 00:12:07.480
is starting to nibble away at certain segments

00:12:07.480 --> 00:12:10.020
where Tesla used to have things mostly to itself.

00:12:10.580 --> 00:12:12.580
And over on the forums and social channels,

00:12:12.580 --> 00:12:17.060
there's active debate about how the refreshed BMW iX-3

00:12:17.060 --> 00:12:20.420
compares to the model Y in real-world range,

00:12:20.720 --> 00:12:23.180
ride quality, and long-term ownership costs.

00:12:23.460 --> 00:12:24.740
These conversations are healthy.

00:12:25.360 --> 00:12:28.140
They remind us that while Tesla still leads in many areas,

00:12:28.460 --> 00:12:30.860
software charging access, resale value,

00:12:31.400 --> 00:12:34.580
traditional luxury brands have closed some of the gaps on fit

00:12:34.580 --> 00:12:37.200
and finish and pure driving dynamics.

00:12:37.600 --> 00:12:39.100
Staying honest about those areas

00:12:39.100 --> 00:12:40.960
is the only way the company keeps improving.

00:12:41.600 --> 00:12:43.620
Finally, it's worth taking a step back

00:12:43.620 --> 00:12:46.700
and applying some first principles thinking to battery chemistry.

00:12:46.960 --> 00:12:49.940
Because the mainstream commentary often oversimplifies it.

00:12:50.080 --> 00:12:52.240
A lot of people treat lithium iron phosphate

00:12:52.240 --> 00:12:55.020
versus nickel manganese cobalt as a simple binary,

00:12:55.320 --> 00:12:57.980
cheap, and safe versus expensive and energy dense.

00:12:58.580 --> 00:13:00.720
The engineering reality is far more nuanced.

00:13:01.140 --> 00:13:03.060
Different vehicles have different duty cycles,

00:13:03.380 --> 00:13:05.180
weight targets, and longevity requirements,

00:13:05.440 --> 00:13:07.560
which means the optimal chemistry can change

00:13:07.560 --> 00:13:09.440
depending on whether you're building a heavy truck,

00:13:09.440 --> 00:13:12.580
a long-range sedan, or a cost-sensitive compact SUV.

00:13:13.220 --> 00:13:15.080
LFP cells have come a long way.

00:13:15.500 --> 00:13:18.240
They now deliver roughly 80% of the energy density

00:13:18.240 --> 00:13:20.920
of older nickel-based packs while exposing the company

00:13:20.920 --> 00:13:23.400
to about half the raw material cost volatility.

00:13:23.880 --> 00:13:26.300
That makes them attractive for certain applications.

00:13:27.040 --> 00:13:29.500
But physics still favors nickel-based cathodes

00:13:29.500 --> 00:13:30.720
when every kilogram matters

00:13:30.720 --> 00:13:33.380
like in passenger cars chasing maximum range.

00:13:33.900 --> 00:13:35.440
Iron phosphate, on the other hand,

00:13:35.500 --> 00:13:36.760
can shine on cycle life

00:13:36.760 --> 00:13:38.340
with many packs now demonstrating

00:13:38.340 --> 00:13:41.180
well over 3,000 deep cycles before dropping

00:13:41.180 --> 00:13:42.600
to 80% capacity.

00:13:43.200 --> 00:13:46.160
Tesla's 4680 cell format adds another layer

00:13:46.160 --> 00:13:47.580
by cutting inactive material

00:13:47.580 --> 00:13:49.140
and improving thermal performance

00:13:49.140 --> 00:13:50.880
no matter which cathode you choose.

00:13:51.480 --> 00:13:54.380
Something a lot of analysts still treat as a minor detail

00:13:54.380 --> 00:13:56.120
rather than a fundamental advantage.

00:13:56.700 --> 00:13:58.740
The real insight is that Tesla designs

00:13:58.740 --> 00:14:00.040
from the physics outward,

00:14:00.700 --> 00:14:02.480
maximizing silicon in the anodes,

00:14:02.640 --> 00:14:04.520
removing cobalt wherever it makes sense,

00:14:04.520 --> 00:14:06.180
and engineering pack architecture

00:14:06.180 --> 00:14:09.100
so the same production lines can handle multiple chemistries.

00:14:09.520 --> 00:14:11.720
That flexibility lets them shift emphasis

00:14:11.720 --> 00:14:13.920
as material prices or regulations change

00:14:13.920 --> 00:14:15.940
without retooling entire platforms.

00:14:16.480 --> 00:14:19.860
The popular narrative that Tesla is all in on one chemistry

00:14:19.860 --> 00:14:22.040
or forever stuck with expensive materials

00:14:22.040 --> 00:14:23.780
misses how they treat chemistry

00:14:23.780 --> 00:14:26.760
as one variable inside a much larger system.

00:14:27.660 --> 00:14:29.080
That system's level thinking

00:14:29.080 --> 00:14:31.580
is exactly why their approach keeps diverging

00:14:31.580 --> 00:14:33.500
from competitors who tend to lock

00:14:33.500 --> 00:14:35.420
into a single chemistry strategy

00:14:35.420 --> 00:14:37.160
and then hope the market behaves.

00:14:37.700 --> 00:14:39.700
Before we wrap up, definitely keep an ear

00:14:39.700 --> 00:14:41.040
on the upcoming earnings call.

00:14:41.740 --> 00:14:43.560
The comments around factory utilization,

00:14:43.880 --> 00:14:45.220
the real progress on FSD

00:14:45.220 --> 00:14:47.100
and how the semi ramp is tracking

00:14:47.100 --> 00:14:49.260
will matter more than any single stock move.

